{%- doc -%}
  Restores content the 2026 theme migration dropped from individual Brew Guides blog articles:
  the full brew recipe (intro + spec table) and the "running low on this coffee?" cross-sell
  back to the matching product. The Tastes Like / Roast info that used to sit near the title is
  blocks/brewguide-article-tasting-info.liquid, nested inside main-blog-post's own block_order
  right after the title block -- see that file's doc comment for why it's a block there rather
  than a second sibling section like this one. This section renders after the full article body
  (main-blog-post), where the old recipe block and cross-sell sat too.

  Source: sections/article-template.liquid in Shopify theme 147471433919 ("ONA - OLD
  11/26/2025"), the last pre-cutover snapshot -- a straight content port adapted to this
  theme's own components (dl/dt/dd spec list matching blocks/product-brew-guide.liquid, CSS
  custom properties, {% stylesheet %}), not a copy of the old pre-Horizon markup (inline
  <style>, decorative animated SVG, raw <table>s).

  Scoped to the Brew Guides blog only (blog.handle == 'brewguides') so it stays inert on
  News/Journal/other blogs that share templates/article.json. Gates on the global `blog` object,
  not `article.blog.handle`: a live diagnostic (PR #286/#287, an unconditional debug comment
  printing both) proved `article.blog.handle` resolves BLANK in this theme's actual rendering of
  this page -- both here and in the sibling blocks/brewguide-article-tasting-info.liquid, so it
  isn't specific to either file -- even though `article.metafields.*` on the very same object
  reads correctly. `blog` is the simpler, standard Liquid context object for "the blog the
  current page belongs to" and resolved correctly in the same diagnostic.

  Metafield types, see reference_shopify_json_string_metafield_no_value:
    - accentuate.content_brew_guide   string (raw HTML)            -> no .value, read directly
    - accentuate.title_item           json_string (array)          -> no .value, read directly
    - accentuate.content_item         json_string (array)          -> no .value, read directly
    - accentuate.select_product       string (a product handle)    -> no .value, read directly

  Cross-sell lookup: the old section looped every product in collections.all comparing handles,
  which is an O(n) scan on every page render for what is a direct key lookup. all_products[handle]
  does the same match via a hash lookup instead -- same matching behaviour (both return nothing
  when accentuate.select_product is blank or points at a handle that no longer exists), no loop.
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign is_brewguide_article = false
  if blog.handle == 'brewguides'
    assign is_brewguide_article = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if is_brewguide_article -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign guide_intro = article.metafields.accentuate.content_brew_guide

    # The mock sets this heading's second half in italics. The copy lives in the metafield,
    # not in the theme, and all 24 Brew Guides carry the identical pasted string -- so the
    # emphasis has to be applied on the way out. The replace is inert the moment an author
    # writes anything else there, which is the point: it adds a flourish to known copy and
    # never rewrites copy it does not recognise.
    assign guide_intro = guide_intro | replace: 'How to get the best tasting cup', 'How to get the <em>best tasting cup</em>'

    assign spec_labels = article.metafields.accentuate.title_item
    assign spec_values = article.metafields.accentuate.content_item

    # The two lists are maintained by hand on the article (same discipline as
    # blocks/product-brew-guide.liquid's product-level pair), so pair only as far as both go
    # rather than indexing past the end of the shorter one and printing empty rows.
    assign spec_count = 0
    if spec_labels.size > 0 and spec_values.size > 0
      assign spec_count = spec_labels.size
      if spec_values.size < spec_count
        assign spec_count = spec_values.size
      endif
    endif

    assign cross_sell_handle = article.metafields.accentuate.select_product
    assign cross_sell_product = blank
    if cross_sell_handle != blank
      assign cross_sell_product = all_products[cross_sell_handle]
    endif
  -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Codex, PR #282: spec_count > 0 only means both lists have that many entries, not that any
    row actually survives the label/value blank guard below -- if every value happened to be
    blank or whitespace, spec_count would still be > 0, has_recipe would be set, and the page
    would render a standalone "Brew guide" heading over an empty <dl>. Captured and tested
    directly here instead, same pattern as blocks/product-faqs.liquid's faq_items capture: one
    test against the markup actually produced, not a count that can't see into it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture spec_rows -%}
    {%- if spec_count > 0 -%}
      {%- assign last_index = spec_count | minus: 1 -%}
      {%- for index in (0..last_index) -%}
        {%- assign label = spec_labels[index] | strip -%}
        {%- assign value = spec_values[index] | strip -%}
        {%- if label != blank and value != blank -%}
          <div class="brewguide-article-recipe__row">
            <dt class="brewguide-article-recipe__label">{{ label }}</dt>
            <dd class="brewguide-article-recipe__value">{{ value }}</dd>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}

  {%- liquid
    assign has_recipe = false
    if guide_intro != blank or spec_rows != blank
      assign has_recipe = true
    endif

    assign has_any_content = false
    if has_recipe or cross_sell_product != blank
      assign has_any_content = true
    endif
  -%}

  {%- if has_any_content -%}
    {%- if has_recipe -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The band is deliberately NOT inside .section. In the mock the brew guide table is a
        full-bleed maroon panel, and .section is Horizon's page-width grid -- a child of it is
        placed in the centred content column, which is what kept this block to a 736px measure
        on a 1363px page. The band is a plain child of the section wrapper instead, so it
        reaches the viewport edge the way Horizon's own full-width sections do, and the measure
        is restored inside it by .brewguide-article-recipe's own max-width.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="brewguide-article-recipe-band">
        <div class="brewguide-article-recipe">
          <h2 class="brewguide-article-recipe__heading">Brew guide</h2>
          {%- if guide_intro != blank -%}
            <div class="brewguide-article-recipe__intro rte">{{ guide_intro }}</div>
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if spec_rows != blank -%}
            <dl class="brewguide-article-recipe__spec">{{ spec_rows }}</dl>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if cross_sell_product != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        .section is Horizon's own page-width grid wrapper (assets/base.css): a direct child of
        it is auto-placed in the centred content column with the theme's standard gutters.
        Without it this card would render edge-to-edge against the viewport, since it isn't
        nested inside the existing main-blog-post section -- it's a sibling top-level section in
        templates/article.json, so it needs its own instance of that wrapper. The recipe band
        above wants the opposite and so stays outside it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="section">
        <div class="brewguide-article-cross-sell">
          {%- if cross_sell_product.featured_media != blank -%}
            <a href="{{ cross_sell_product.url }}" class="brewguide-article-cross-sell__image" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">
              {{
                cross_sell_product.featured_media
                | image_url: width: 300
                | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', widths: '150, 300'
              }}
            </a>
          {%- endif -%}
          <div class="brewguide-article-cross-sell__content">
            <h3 class="brewguide-article-cross-sell__heading">Running low on this coffee?</h3>
            <p class="brewguide-article-cross-sell__title">{{ cross_sell_product.title }}</p>
            <a href="{{ cross_sell_product.url }}" class="button brewguide-article-cross-sell__link">Buy another bag</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  /*
   * The mock's "Brew guide table" is a full-bleed maroon panel in the palette's two extremes,
   * not in the page's own scheme -- so the band sets both ground and text from
   * --palette-darkest/--palette-lightest (snippets/color-palette.liquid), the same pair
   * snippets/contrast-override.liquid and snippets/header-megamenu.liquid already resolve
   * against. Everything inside inherits that cream, which is why the rules below can hand
   * `inherit` to the pasted metafield markup instead of naming a colour twice.
   */
  .brewguide-article-recipe-band {
    background-color: var(--palette-darkest);
    color: var(--palette-lightest);
    padding-block: var(--gap-3xl);
    padding-inline: var(--gap-lg);
    margin-block: var(--gap-3xl);
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--gap-lg);
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /*
   * "Brew guide" is the mock's eyebrow, not its headline -- 12px uppercase above the serif
   * title, where it used to render as a 24px Silk Serif heading competing with it. It stays
   * an <h2> so the band keeps a heading in the document outline; only its presentation
   * changes. --font-h6--size is 0.75rem, exactly the mock's 12px.
   */
  .brewguide-article-recipe__heading {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-h6--size);
    font-family: var(--font-body--family);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /*
   * The intro is a metafield whose HTML was pasted out of the pre-Horizon site. All 24 Brew
   * Guides carry byte-identical markup: an <h3> in Karla at 50px in grey rgb(43,44,48), and a
   * note in 13px "Karla Bold" with 3px tracking -- both sitting on the pale blue
   * rgb(189,214,230) highlight the paste brought with it, every value in an inline style
   * attribute and flagged data-pasted="true".
   *
   * Inline styles outrank a stylesheet, so neutralising them needs !important. Every rule here
   * is scoped inside .brewguide-article-recipe__intro, so nothing outside this one container
   * is affected, and each hands presentation back to a theme token rather than to another
   * fixed value -- anything an author writes here in future inherits the band instead.
   *
   * The real fix is on the content side: clearing the pasted styles out of
   * accentuate.content_brew_guide. Until that happens this keeps the band readable.
   */
  .brewguide-article-recipe__intro :is(h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6, p, span, strong, em, li, a) {
    font-family: inherit !important;
    color: inherit !important;
    background-color: transparent !important;
    max-width: none !important;
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__intro :is(h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6) {
    margin: 0 !important;
    font-family: var(--font-heading--family) !important;
    font-size: var(--font-h2--size) !important;
    font-weight: 400 !important;
    line-height: 1.12 !important;
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__intro p {
    margin: 0 !important;
    font-size: var(--font-paragraph--size) !important;
    line-height: 1.45 !important;
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__intro p :is(span, strong, em) {
    font-size: inherit !important;
    font-weight: inherit !important;
    letter-spacing: normal !important;
  }

  /*
   * The note reads "NOTE THESE ARE A STARTING POINT..." -- the capitals are in the stored copy
   * itself, not a text-transform, so sentence case (what the mock shows) has to be recovered
   * rather than simply switched off. Lower-casing the paragraph and restoring its first letter
   * is safe for this exact sentence, which carries no proper noun; it is a presentation
   * workaround for pasted copy, and the content-side fix above retires it.
   */
  .brewguide-article-recipe__intro p,
  .brewguide-article-recipe__intro p :is(span, strong, em) {
    text-transform: lowercase !important;
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__intro p::first-letter {
    text-transform: uppercase !important;
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__spec {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    border-block-start: 1px solid rgb(var(--palette-lightest-rgb) / 0.55);
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--gap-sm);
    padding-block: var(--gap-md);
    border-block-end: 1px solid rgb(var(--palette-lightest-rgb) / 0.55);
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__label {
    font-size: var(--font-h6--size);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .brewguide-article-recipe__value {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-paragraph--size);
    text-align: end;
  }

  .brewguide-article-cross-sell {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--gap-lg);
    padding: var(--padding-xl);
    border: 1px solid rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / var(--opacity-10-25));
    border-radius: var(--style-border-radius-popover);
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .brewguide-article-cross-sell__image {
    display: block;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 96px;
  }

  .brewguide-article-cross-sell__image img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--style-border-radius-popover);
  }

  .brewguide-article-cross-sell__content {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--gap-xs);
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 12rem;
  }

  .brewguide-article-cross-sell__heading {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-h6--size);
  }

  .brewguide-article-cross-sell__title {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-foreground-subdued);
  }

  .brewguide-article-cross-sell__link {
    justify-self: start;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Brew guide article info",
  "settings": [],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Brew guide article info"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
